//? Title: Sum the odds to one: stopping on the last success
//? Subtitle: Actual suffix decisions, certain events and fixed-threshold probability curves
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Solve the independent last-success stopping problem with the canonical odds
producer. Tiny hand-checkable events come first, followed by the original ten
probabilities and secretary-model controls. Actual reverse traversal is
distinguished from the separate computation of all threshold probabilities.
)

\toc

> What constitutes a win

Let X_i be mutually independent Bernoulli events with known probabilities p_i.
After observing each event, either continue or stop irreversibly at a success. A
win means stopping at the final success in the entire sequence. Never
encountering an accepted success loses. Arbitrary dependence, estimated
probabilities and unknown future probabilities are outside this model.

For a fixed first eligible index s, the strategy accepts the first subsequent
success. It wins exactly when the suffix contains one success. Write Q_s for
zero successes and W_s for exactly one. Prepending p to a suffix gives

$$ Q_{new}=(1-p)Q, \qquad W_{new}=pQ+(1-p)W. $$
\alttext(Zero successes require the new event to fail; exactly one success arises either from a new success followed by none, or a new failure followed by one.)

The empty suffix starts Q=1,W=0. This recurrence also handles p=1 without
division. When all suffix probabilities are below1,

$$ W_s=Q_sR_s, \qquad R_s=\sum_{i=s}^{n}\frac{p_i}{1-p_i}. $$
\alttext(The chance of exactly one suffix success is the suffix failure product times the sum of its success odds.)

Comparing adjacent thresholds gives W_s−W_{s+1}=p_s Q_{s+1}(1−R_{s+1}).
Extending a suffix improves its win probability until the later suffix odds
exceed1. Thus the odds rule scans backwards and stops when the accumulated odds
reach1, or a certain event is encountered; if neither occurs it uses the full
sequence. The usual independence argument also establishes optimality among
adaptive stopping rules, not just this threshold family. Equality can give
multiple equally good thresholds, so a plotted maximum need not identify a
unique index.

> Three half-probability events

For[.5,.5,.5], the producer visits only the last event: its odds equal1
immediately, so threshold3 gives win probability.5. Threshold2 has the same.5
probability. The first event is never visited by the suffix loop, although every
input probability is validated. The diagram and table use the actual single
record.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/odds_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t = dm::algorithms::odds_algorithm_trace({.5, .5, .5});
    auto &s = t.steps[0];
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("empty", {0, 0},
           std::format("Empty suffix\nQ={} W={}", double(s.none_before),
                       double(s.one_before)));
    d.node("event", {7, 0},
           std::format("Actual event {}\np={} odds={}", s.event, s.probability,
                       double(s.odds)));
    d.node("stop", {14, 0},
           std::format("Actual threshold {}\nwin probability {}",
                       t.run.result.threshold, t.run.result.win_probability));
    d.edge("empty", "event").edge("event", "stop");
    std::print("| visited event | p | actual finite odds sum | actual suffix Q | actual suffix W | stop " "reached |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (auto &s : t.steps)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", s.event, s.probability,
                   double(s.sum_after), double(s.none_after),
                   double(s.one_after), s.reached);
    d.save_svg("build/odds_tiny.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| visited event | p | actual finite odds sum | actual suffix Q | actual suffix W | stop reached |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |
| 3 | 0.5 | 1 | 0.5 | 0.5 | true |
\image(figures/odds_tiny.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual backwards visit and stopping decision for three half-probability events.)
\alttext(Only event3 is visited by the suffix producer; full input validation is a separate operation.)

> The original ten known probabilities

The original vector is[.10,.15,.05,.30,.20,.40,.25,.35,.12,.28]. It is a fixed
illustrative instance, rather than a random sample or a labeled dataset. The
canonical curve helper computes every fixed-threshold W_s through the suffix
recurrence, including the never-accept threshold n+1. Those curve rows are not
falsely presented as visits made by the early-stopping producer.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/odds_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto inputs = dm::algorithms::odds_algorithm_demo_probabilities();
    auto t = dm::algorithms::odds_algorithm_trace(inputs);
    auto curve = dm::algorithms::odds_threshold_curve(inputs);
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("Actual selected threshold: {}. Actual win probability: {:.12f}. Actual visited suffix " "events: {}.\n\n",
               t.run.result.threshold, t.run.result.win_probability,
               t.run.visits);
    std::print("| fixed threshold | actual computed win probability | actual no-success probability |\n| " "---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (auto &r : curve) {
        x.push_back(r.threshold);
        y.push_back(r.win_probability);
        std::print("| {} | {:.12f} | {:.12f} |\n", r.threshold,
                   r.win_probability, r.no_success_probability);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.lines(x, y, "threshold curve", dm::graphics::RGBA{37, 99, 235, 255}).points(x, y, dm::graphics::RGBA{37, 99, 235, 255}, 3.).abline_v(t.run.result.threshold, dm::graphics::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2);
    p.title("Original ten events: fixed-threshold win probabilities").x_label(
        "one-based first eligible event").y_label("probability of stopping on last success").y_limits(0, .55).save_svg("build/odds_original.svg");
}
```
